{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794185261,"nonce":"xFlboWLp/tWjJyb2VbArB2yFjZ4=","message":"Owner direction 2026-11-09: a proper front end, not WGSL text checked by naga's frontend. Pipeline:\n- syn AST, then a resolved IR (hir) built with a string interner (Symbol) for every identifier and a hash-consed type arena (TypeId); expressions, statements and locals live in per-body arenas, shadowing handled by LocalId rather than renaming.\n- Analyzer: name resolution over module and block scopes; bidirectional type inference with WGSL's abstract int and float literals concretised by context; operator rules (vector and scalar mixing, matrix products, comparisons to bool vectors, bitwise and shifts); builtin signatures (methods and free functions); struct literals by field name; assignment only to mutable places (let mut, private, workgroup, read_write storage); call graph ordered for naga, recursion rejected.\n- Rules: entry-point IO (vertex returns a position builtin, IO is bound scalars and vectors, unique locations, compute has a workgroup size and no return), resource bindings unique and host-shareable, and Rust's conventions as warnings (snake_case functions and locals, UpperCamelCase types, SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE constants, unused bindings, unreachable code). A proc macro cannot emit warnings on stable, so russl! surfaces them through a deprecated item at the span; the CLI and russl_build print them.\n- Lowering straight to naga::Module (types with WGSL layout offsets, globals, constants, overrides, functions, entry points), each naga span an index into the source-span table so validation errors map back to the Rust token.\nThen validation and every target, as before.","files":null}]}